# Add or implement a tool

A tool has two halves. `armature.yaml` declares it (name, description, side effect, args) and
Armature generates the argument model, registry entry and framework wiring. You write
`src/{{package}}/tools/<name>.py`, which Armature creates once and never overwrites.
Framework details are in `references/{{framework}}.md`.

## 1. Declare it in armature.yaml

```yaml
capabilities:
  custom_tools:
    - name: lookup_order            # verb_noun, snake_case, unique
      description: Look up an order by id and return its status, items and total.
      side_effect: read             # none | read | write | destructive
      timeout_s: 30
      auth_env: SHOP_API_TOKEN      # only if it calls an authenticated service
      args:
        - name: order_id
          type: string              # string | integer | number | boolean
          description: The order id, e.g. "1234".
```

Then run `armature generate`. It creates `tools/<name>.py`, updates `runtime/tool_args.py`
and adds a row to the tool tables. Add the tool's section to `docs/TOOL_CONTRACTS.md` by hand.

## 2. Name and describe it for the model

- The description says what the tool does, when to use it and what it returns, in one or two
  sentences. Mention when *not* to use it if a neighbouring tool is similar.
- Argument names are unambiguous (`order_id`, not `id`) and every argument has a description
  with an example value.
- Prefer one tool that does a job completely over several that must be chained.

## 3. Pick the side-effect class honestly

| Class | Use when the tool | Approval by default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `none` | computes something, touches nothing | no |
| `read` | reads from a system | no |
| `write` | creates or changes something | yes |
| `destructive` | deletes, sends, pays, or cannot be undone | yes |

In eval mode write and destructive tools are always stubbed, whatever the approval policy.

## 4. Implement run()

```python
async def run(args: LookupOrderArgs) -> Any:
    ...
```

- `args` is already validated. Return JSON-serialisable data; it becomes the tool result.
- Keep results small: return the fields the model needs, not whole API payloads. Large
  results cost tokens and bury the answer.
- Raise an exception with a short, actionable message when the call fails
  (`raise ValueError("order 1234 not found; check the id")`). The runtime turns it into a tool
  error the model can read and recover from; it does not crash the run.
- Read credentials from the environment variable in `auth_env`. Never hard-code secrets.
- Long calls are cut off at `timeout_s`. Use async HTTP clients so a timeout really stops the
  work.

## 5. Test it

Add a test to `tests/test_tools.py` that calls the handler directly with real arguments and
asserts on the result, plus one for the failure path. Mock the external service; never call a
real write or destructive endpoint from tests. Then run `uv run pytest`.

## 6. Record it

Update the tool's row in `docs/BUILD_STATUS.md`: `IMPLEMENTED` once a test covers the real
code path, `REQUIRES_AUTH` if it cannot be tested without a credential.
